output.text:
{"connection_id": "BC-f078151d788dedf2b5fc", "ref": "Augustine, Confessions 10.8.12", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "fields and palaces of memory", "note": "Augustine's image of memory as a vast interior storehouse gives a Christian interpretive companion to the commentary's container imagery for what is held, retrieved, or dropped."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6e361c8816004930bf02", "ref": "Augustine, Confessions X.18.27", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "searching memory for what has been lost", "note": "The commentary defines recollection as seeking what has slipped away; Augustine describes searching memory for a lost item, giving that definition an introspective account."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f9290d60cc5d50cab6bf", "ref": "Augustine, Confessions X.8.12", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "memory as capacious halls and treasuries", "note": "The commentary pictures memory as a sequence of containers holding what is entrusted; Augustine describes memory as immense halls containing innumerable images, a close account of memory as a storehouse."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7f0ad03e9bfe99cd623c", "ref": "Basil, On the Holy Spirit 27.66", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "written teaching and unwritten apostolic tradition", "note": "Basil distinguishes church teaching received from writing from teaching transmitted through unwritten apostolic tradition, a later Christian framework that complicates any simple equation of preserved revelation with pages alone."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7c1be4fdf9a9a5b70182", "ref": "Eusebius, Ecclesiastical History 3.39.4", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Papias prefers the living voice to information from books", "note": "Papias' reported preference for remembered living testimony over books gives a Christian counter-emphasis: the commentary joins memory to sheets, whereas this reception tradition privileges living recall over written repositories."}
{"connection_id": "BC-31a379152b2170a0dceb", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.3", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Paul delivers what he received, according to Scripture", "note": "The commentary notes a secondary sense of transmitting a report from another; Paul says he handed on what he had received and frames the message as occurring according to Scripture."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6eda2d575174884b54ea", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.3", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "what was received is delivered onward", "note": "Paul's received-and-delivered formula is a precise early Christian transmission chain for the commentary's sense of passing on a report from another."}
{"connection_id": "BC-eafaa4432c336fae0cac", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.8", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "each receives wages according to personal labor", "note": "Paul's reward according to one's own labor closely parallels the commentary's cited content that a person has only what he has striven for."}
{"connection_id": "BC-aa5cfc0e84e0c5ce3593", "ref": "SBLGNT:1John.2.17", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "world passes; the one doing God's will remains", "note": "The commentary contrasts the preferred worldly life with an afterlife that lasts; John says the world and its desire are passing, while the one who does God's will remains forever."}
{"connection_id": "BC-638da4caef4b8dfc6933", "ref": "SBLGNT:1John.2.17", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "the world passes; God's doer abides forever", "note": "The world and its desire pass away while the doer of God's will remains forever, directly matching the contrast of worldly preference and endurance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7a8079ce3b637ce5d496", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.25", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "the Lord's word endures and is preached", "note": "The Petrine reuse of the enduring-word formula identifies that lasting word with the gospel preached to its readers, combining permanence and proclamation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e313dd1d240588bfb6cd", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.4.13", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "public reading, exhortation, and teaching", "note": "The triad makes public reading serve exhortation and teaching, placing Christian practice beside the commentary's joined recitation and reminder."}
{"connection_id": "BC-12c287d37e1a8aa721b6", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.3.3", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "community is a letter written by the Spirit on hearts", "note": "The commentary joins human retention to written pages; Paul calls the community a letter of Christ written not in ink but by the Spirit on human hearts."}
{"connection_id": "BC-08492587cc282999e46e", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.3.3", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Christ's letter written on hearts", "note": "Paul contrasts ink and stone with the Spirit's writing on human hearts, a Christian reworking of the commentary's relation between held memory and written sheets."}
{"connection_id": "BC-60bef6f7db26a56a6277", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.4.18", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "visible things are temporary, unseen things eternal", "note": "The commentary contrasts the chosen present life with what endures; Paul distinguishes what is seen and temporary from what is unseen and eternal."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b1dfd4b634039082e609", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.4.18", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "seen things temporary; unseen things eternal", "note": "The visible-temporary versus invisible-eternal contrast states the same valuation pattern developed around 87:16-17."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6e488ea00c66fc4b5fae", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.5.10", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "each receives what was done in the body", "note": "Universal appearance before Christ's judgment seat gives the individual recompense setting behind the commentary's sheets-of-Moses-and-Abraham ethics."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8f685c85cc1179fa74a1", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Pet.1.13", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "stirring readers by way of reminder", "note": "The commentary says reminder brings a needed matter back to mind; Peter explicitly describes his writing as stirring readers up by reminding them while he is still alive."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7269f0c4d6aeebce99ea", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Pet.1.15", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "recall after the speaker's departure", "note": "The commentary connects reminder with retrieving what has passed and pages that preserve teaching; Peter writes so his readers can recall these things after his death."}
{"connection_id": "BC-98f48a38a0398f352b5f", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Pet.1.15", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "provision for recall after the speaker's departure", "note": "The author seeks to ensure that readers can recall the teaching after his death, making durable remembrance a stated literary purpose."}
{"connection_id": "BC-68e8ed7d25f221ffe20a", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Pet.3.2", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "remember prophets' words and apostolic command", "note": "The letter explicitly asks readers to recall former prophetic speech and the apostolic command, joining old writings and present exhortation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6cc7c6c998caa4c5ef61", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Thess.2.15", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hold teaching received by spoken word or letter", "note": "The commentary traces recitation into written pages and notes a root sense of transmitted report; Paul tells the community to hold traditions delivered either orally or by letter."}
{"connection_id": "BC-db28f1707809a02eade2", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Thess.2.15", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hold traditions taught by word or letter", "note": "The command explicitly joins oral teaching and written letter as forms of retained tradition, directly comparable to recitation becoming pages."}
{"connection_id": "BC-861702a5b17d3eaacd60", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.1.14", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "guard the good deposit entrusted to you", "note": "The commentary defines forgetting as failing to keep what was entrusted; Paul tells Timothy to guard the entrusted deposit through the Holy Spirit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dda45173d61fd367e966", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.1.14", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "guard the good deposit", "note": "The entrusted teaching is to be guarded through the Holy Spirit, a close Christian analogue to the commentary's definition of forgetting as failing to hold an entrusted thing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-743e9f5c9b4899a36138", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.2.2", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "heard teaching entrusted through further teachers", "note": "What Timothy heard is entrusted to reliable people who will teach others, making preservation a multi-generation oral chain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1d6c74818b228e989b55", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.3.15", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Timothy has known sacred writings since childhood", "note": "The commentary identifies Moses' pages as a source of teaching; Paul points to Timothy's childhood familiarity with sacred writings and their power to make him wise for salvation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c5e29f26fd187bfe9584", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.4.13", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "books and especially parchments", "note": "Paul's request for books and parchments gives a concrete Christian reference for the commentary's material sheets of skin or parchment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f09dbb414fc4a7f833a3", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.15.21", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Moses read in synagogues every Sabbath from ancient generations", "note": "James describes Moses as publicly read every Sabbath in city synagogues, a New Testament witness to the continuing recitation of an ancient Mosaic written legacy."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c58e4f808ef6d4dc44ca", "ref": "SBLGNT:Col.3.16", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "word dwelling richly through teaching and admonition", "note": "The indwelling word produces reciprocal teaching and admonition, a communal version of 87:9's beneficial reminder."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d3d6379444b198b82e10", "ref": "SBLGNT:Col.4.16", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "letters read and exchanged between churches", "note": "A letter is read in one church and exchanged for reading in another, a concrete case of written teaching being circulated rather than left as an isolated page."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3653a99d16ee72468493", "ref": "SBLGNT:Gal.3.17", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Abrahamic covenant temporally distinguished from later law", "note": "Paul's argument separates the Abrahamic promise from the law arriving 430 years later, a different ordering of Abraham and Moses that must remain visible beside 87:18-19's paired sheets."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3d80b65865e18e0d45ab", "ref": "SBLGNT:Gal.3.8", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Scripture announces good news beforehand to Abraham", "note": "The commentary says its message is already in Abraham's and Moses' pages; Paul reads Abraham's promise as Scripture's prior announcement of the gospel to the nations."}
{"connection_id": "BC-71e9597e992f3f86de6b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Gal.3.8", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Scripture proclaims good news beforehand to Abraham", "note": "Paul reads the Abrahamic promise as Scripture's advance proclamation of the gospel; it supplies a Christian interpretive analogue, rather than literal Abrahamic pages, for ancient Abrahamic revelation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-81905f4f1eed2690253a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Gal.6.2", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "command to bear one another's burdens", "note": "The commentary quotes a rule against one person carrying another's burden; Paul commands mutual burden-bearing, a real ethical counterpoint whose context is helping someone caught in transgression."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9d9f999ad95e55ae2ca9", "ref": "SBLGNT:Gal.6.5", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "each person carries their own load", "note": "The commentary cites the rule that no bearer carries another's burden; Paul says each person will carry their own load, though his surrounding discussion distinguishes this from helping others in trouble."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5533229b0af05f0a4a43", "ref": "SBLGNT:Gal.6.5", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "each person bears his own load", "note": "Paul's individual load supplies a direct New Testament comparator to the commentary's cited earlier-sheet claim that no bearer bears another's burden."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c7d6c3595a8d175f8e05", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.11.26", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Moses values reward above Egypt's treasure", "note": "The commentary names Moses after rejecting worldly preference; Hebrews says Moses counted reproach greater wealth than Egypt's treasures because he looked to the reward."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a220f2b33a3aeb441290", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.11.26", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Moses values future reward above Egypt's wealth", "note": "Moses judges reproach for the Messiah greater wealth than Egypt's treasures because he looks to reward, a particularly apt Mosaic comparison for 87:16-19."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cf80f7cb30db0d3de4ff", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.6.10", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "God does not forget faithful work", "note": "God's refusal to forget service makes divine non-forgetting an ethical and judicial claim beside the commentary's Lord who neither strays nor forgets."}

{"connection_id": "BC-fd0dbdd67ddd16e323f5", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.8.10", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "law placed in minds and written on hearts", "note": "The commentary moves between retained memory and pages; Hebrews quotes a covenant promise that God's laws will be placed in minds and written on hearts."}
{"connection_id": "BC-327d6fa305bfd102d67a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.8.10", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "laws put in minds and written on hearts", "note": "The new-covenant citation makes divine law both mentally placed and internally written, closely paralleling the commentary's movement from memory to inscription."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1f511998a4ac1caa4c2b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.24", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hearer looks and then forgets what he was like", "note": "The commentary warns that a received thing can be let go; James's forgetful hearer leaves the mirror and immediately forgets, making departure from instruction concrete."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f25d3b03162f562624c8", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.24", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "forgetful hearer after looking", "note": "James' hearer immediately forgets after seeing himself, a specific Christian image of received perception that is not retained or acted on."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cf4e1802b9fe74227873", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.25", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "persevering doer looks into the law and is blessed", "note": "The commentary links retained remembrance to action; James contrasts the forgetful hearer with one who perseveres in the law and acts, who is blessed."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6b0c99050ada4b90c1cd", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.14.26", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Spirit teaches and reminds disciples of Jesus' words", "note": "The commentary joins divine teaching to the promise of not forgetting; Jesus says the Spirit will teach the disciples and remind them of what he said."}
{"connection_id": "BC-82acd421bb9afaf2eea6", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.14.26", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Spirit teaches and reminds disciples", "note": "The Paraclete will teach all things and bring Jesus' words back to memory, directly pairing instruction with divinely secured recollection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-db5d3d262a054d9dda8d", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.5.46", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Moses wrote about Jesus", "note": "The commentary places its present message in Moses' pages; Jesus interprets Moses' writing as testimony about him, a distinct Christian claim about what those pages contain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-85da1f90c09bedf0d117", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.5.46", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Moses wrote of Jesus", "note": "Jesus' claim that Moses wrote about him is a Christian rereading of Mosaic writing that changes what a reader finds in the pages named in 87:19."}
{"connection_id": "BC-59072ddf2f387c817cba", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.6.12", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "gather leftover fragments so none is lost", "note": "The commentary pictures forgotten matter as dropped remnants and traces a movement toward gathering; Jesus orders the leftover fragments gathered so that none is lost."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4e8fd230f32f5df102b2", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.1.2", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "eyewitnesses deliver the word onward", "note": "Luke begins from traditions delivered by eyewitnesses and servants of the word, a narrative account of report transmission before the written Gospel."}
{"connection_id": "BC-387168ac6426db7d2c04", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.1.3", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "orderly written account compiled from earlier testimony", "note": "The commentary's q-r-ء discussion presents reading as gathering elements together; Luke says he carefully followed the received accounts and writes them in order."}
{"connection_id": "BC-284426201a55ce462f47", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.1.3", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "an orderly written account follows received tradition", "note": "After the eyewitness delivery described in Luke 1:2, the author writes an orderly account; the verse supplies the distinct written outcome of the oral transmission already in view."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2b770cce6fd37f320f84", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.1.4", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "written account meant to establish certainty about teaching", "note": "The commentary's pages preserve what was recited; Luke states that his account is written so Theophilus can know the certainty of what he was taught."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3c135238c3fa5b9f040a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.15.8", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "searching for a lost coin until it is found", "note": "The commentary defines recollection as seeking what has slipped away; the woman searches carefully for a lost coin, making recovery an active search rather than passive recall."}
{"connection_id": "BC-94d8416d258f23ba9520", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.22.19", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "ritual meal performed in remembrance", "note": "The commentary places remembrance of the Lord's name immediately before prayer; Jesus tells disciples to repeat a meal in remembrance of him, a distinct Christian liturgical act built around remembrance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-67a3bb5ad7077dbdb5ae", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.22.19", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "do this in remembrance of me", "note": "The meal command turns remembrance into a repeated communal act, a concrete Christian counterpart to the commentary's reminder as an instrument of recall."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fb5fe96029ba0c692b4f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.24.44", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Law, Prophets and Psalms written about Jesus' suffering and rising", "note": "The commentary asserts that its message is found in Abraham's and Moses' pages; Jesus gives a distinct Christian reading in which the wider scriptural corpus points to his passion and resurrection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-26fbbda30f4b935337c0", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.4.16", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Jesus reads Isaiah's scroll in the synagogue", "note": "The commentary connects recited revelation with prophetic pages; Luke depicts Jesus opening Isaiah's scroll and reading it aloud in a public worship setting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e8758a6ac5e5f671b665", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.4.21", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Jesus says the read scripture is fulfilled that day", "note": "The commentary says its message was already present in earlier pages; after reading Isaiah, Jesus interprets the passage as fulfilled in the hearers' present."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c2e048b99be2f9cfd5d2", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.8.12", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "word taken from the heart before it is believed", "note": "The commentary contrasts retained reminder with what is lost; in Jesus' explanation of the seed, the devil removes the word from hearers' hearts so they will not believe."}
{"connection_id": "BC-20656966f8fd47217e34", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.8.13", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "joyful reception without roots ends in falling away", "note": "The commentary says a reminder benefits the one who retains it; Jesus describes hearers who receive the word with joy but fall away when tested because it has no root."}
{"connection_id": "BC-72b3c74062ccc777c387", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.8.14", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "cares, riches and pleasures choke the word", "note": "The commentary rebukes preference for worldly life; Jesus says worldly cares, wealth and pleasures choke the heard word before it matures."}
{"connection_id": "BC-314963da1f14a6ae888a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.8.15", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hearers hold the word fast and bear fruit", "note": "The commentary says the one who fears remembers; Jesus contrasts good soil that holds the heard word fast and bears fruit with the losses in the preceding soils."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5a2755be55e3f1a3b598", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.8.15", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hearers hold the word fast in a good heart", "note": "In the good-soil explanation, hearers retain the word and bear fruit with endurance, a precise Christian image for held rather than dropped revelation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-89048ff74cf70ad12ebc", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.13.31", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Jesus' words will not pass away", "note": "The contrast of passing heaven and earth with imperishable words is a direct New Testament counterpart to the commentary's passing worldly life and retained divine speech."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7effc41c1b9a76f58d63", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.8.36", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "gain the world and forfeit one's life", "note": "Jesus' question about gaining the whole world at the cost of life is a pointed Christian analogue to preferring worldly life over what lasts."}
{"connection_id": "BC-81d67214923c2846aa86", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.10.20", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "the Father's Spirit speaks through disciples", "note": "Jesus assures messengers that the Spirit of their Father speaks through them, a New Testament form of divinely enabled prophetic speech."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5a964e51e3d258f3874f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.16.26", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "gain the whole world yet forfeit one's life", "note": "The commentary rebukes preference for worldly life in light of the lasting hereafter; Jesus asks what profit there is in gaining the whole world at the cost of one's life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6d9cca047a7674c8d3b0", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.19", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "treasure stored on earth is vulnerable to loss", "note": "The commentary condemns preferring worldly life to what lasts; Jesus warns against storing treasure where moth and rust consume and thieves break in."}
{"connection_id": "BC-345b6a24eec396d8c7d2", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.20", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "treasure stored in heaven endures", "note": "The commentary calls the afterlife better and more lasting than worldly life; Jesus contrasts earthly loss with treasure in heaven that is not consumed or stolen."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e84536f8331aae9f9657", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.1.3", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "reader, hearers, and keepers of written prophecy", "note": "Blessing attaches to the reader, hearers, and those who keep a written prophecy's words, tightly joining book, recitation, hearing, and retention."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fbf54b18e2c50304fc78", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.10.10", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "John eats a little scroll before receiving a prophetic task", "note": "The commentary's recitation begins with revelation received by a prophet; John takes a written scroll into himself, sweet in his mouth and bitter in his stomach, before being told to prophesy."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6bb5132148f8f6d1359b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.10.10", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "take and eat the little scroll", "note": "John consumes the revealed scroll, a New Testament image of a written message entering the prophet before further speech."}
