{"connection_id": "BC-1650a89dc5d98f284c9c", "ref": "WLC:Gen.3.6", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Adam takes and eats the forbidden fruit", "note": "Genesis depicts Adam's first transgression as eating fruit in the woman's temptation scene rather than naming forgetfulness; it is the necessary biblical counter-narrative to the commentary's Adam of Q 20:115 who forgets and lacks resolve."}
{"connection_id": "BC-aa7db5d459ea9218f077", "ref": "WLC:Gen.41.51", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew נשה (nashah, forget), a likely cognate of Arabic نسي", "note": "The commentary develops Arabic n-s-y as forgetting; Joseph names Manasseh because God made him forget hardship, likely sharing the Semitic root, though this is positive relief from suffering rather than forgetting revelation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a789a3089dbfdf435e07", "ref": "WLC:Hab.2.2", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "write the vision so a reader can run", "note": "The vision is written plainly on tablets for a reader, an explicit practical reason for fixing revelation in readable written form."}
{"connection_id": "BC-142b0fd5b9cbec2a3e23", "ref": "WLC:Hos.4.6", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "forgotten Torah answered by divine forgetting", "note": "Because the priests have forgotten God's Torah, God says he will forget their children; this directly parallels the commentary's reciprocal forgetting and judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d2d176d151a9e34b7cf8", "ref": "WLC:Isa.29.11", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "sealed book cannot be read", "note": "Isaiah's learned reader cannot read the sealed book, a precise inverse scene to 87:6's divine promise to make the messenger recite and retain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-79a5e3c8303e25f4a00e", "ref": "WLC:Isa.30.8", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "write the oracle on a tablet and in a book as lasting witness", "note": "The commentary carries recited teaching into preserved pages; Isaiah orders a written record for future time, explicitly making inscription a witness that endures."}
{"connection_id": "BC-79e469b3d50a911a3ad0", "ref": "WLC:Isa.30.8", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "write on tablet and book for a future day", "note": "Isaiah is told to inscribe the testimony so it endures as a witness for the future, a specific biblical reason to preserve prophetic speech in writing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d896f6349c617bee0ee0", "ref": "WLC:Isa.34.16", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "read the Lord's book; none of the creatures is missing", "note": "The commentary says collection prevents the recited word from being dropped; Isaiah directs readers to the Lord's book and claims that none of the listed creatures is absent from its account."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2da7fb96b1daa2afc2b6", "ref": "WLC:Isa.34.16", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seek and read YHWH's book; none missing", "note": "In the oracle's announced animal assembly, the reader is sent to YHWH's book and told none will be missing because his Spirit has gathered them; that concrete book-and-gathering image sharpens the commentary's claim that what God collects is not dropped."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b09f4d60ad403088a3f4", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.8", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "grass withers but God's word stands forever", "note": "The contrast between perishing grass and the enduring divine word relates the commentary's passing worldly life to retained and lasting revelation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-347ae49b652c6928ffb2", "ref": "WLC:Isa.49.15", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God promises not to forget Zion", "note": "The commentary's promise is that the Prophet will not forget the recited message; Isaiah reverses the direction, portraying God as the one who will not forget Zion even if a mother could forget her child."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c8315ff0eb199cf593eb", "ref": "WLC:Isa.49.15", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "God will not forget Zion", "note": "The maternal comparison culminates in God's refusal to forget Zion, a personal counterpart to the unforgetting Lord and his record in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-12b3b4da0731c3af6fde", "ref": "WLC:Isa.59.21", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "words never depart from mouths", "note": "The covenant promises that God's words will not depart from the prophet's mouth or his descendants' mouths, a generational form of the no-forgetting promise."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b5aeb8886338c2141e20", "ref": "WLC:Isa.8.16", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "bind testimony and seal Torah among disciples", "note": "The prophet binds and seals testimony among disciples, coupling guarded writing to a community that carries it forward."}

{"connection_id": "BC-54e90758bf89f159c968", "ref": "WLC:Jer.1.9", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "YHWH puts words in Jeremiah's mouth", "note": "The mouth-touching call scene makes prophetic speech a deposited divine word, illuminating 87:6 before the commentary turns to retention."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a12cc38ca748b985d15a", "ref": "WLC:Jer.15.16", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "prophet finds, eats and delights in God's words", "note": "The commentary connects recitation with remembering the Lord's name; Jeremiah says he found and ate God's words and that God's name was invoked over him, joining reception of words to prophetic identity."}
{"connection_id": "BC-af9b9c14bea2192e4b38", "ref": "WLC:Jer.17.10", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "God gives each according to ways and deeds", "note": "YHWH's searching judgment and recompense according to each person's ways supplies a Hebrew Bible counterpart to the commentary's cited earlier-sheet ethics of personal effort and return."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e65b63e0d3ec6d9d450a", "ref": "WLC:Jer.2.32", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Israel forgets God despite the bride comparison", "note": "Jeremiah contrasts a bride who remembers her adornments with Israel that has forgotten God for countless days, making forgetfulness concrete as covenantal abandonment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5cc05ff17af3c10fc942", "ref": "WLC:Jer.31.34", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God remembers sins no more as an act of forgiveness", "note": "The commentary cites divine forgetting as abandonment of the unfaithful; Jeremiah uses not remembering sins as covenant forgiveness, giving divine non-remembrance an opposite moral valence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-53310e5a4f7dd391bb26", "ref": "WLC:Jer.36.2", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "write spoken prophecies on a scroll", "note": "YHWH orders Jeremiah to put all the words spoken against Israel and Judah on a scroll, directly joining oral revelation and a written surface."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2c9f09e482a96e6bebb1", "ref": "WLC:Jer.36.23", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Jehoiakim cuts and burns the scroll", "note": "The commentary describes deliberate forgetting and a promise that recited words will not be lost; the king deliberately destroys the written words, a sharp counter-scene to that assurance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8de596fc15a4ba956fef", "ref": "WLC:Jer.36.23", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "royal burning of the scroll", "note": "Jehoiakim cuts and burns the written columns; its deliberate loss gives a concrete contrary case to the commentary's claim that divine collection is not dropped."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d88457e815234e8f6b4f", "ref": "WLC:Jer.36.28", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "rewrite the burned scroll's former words", "note": "After the scroll is burned, YHWH commands another scroll containing the former words, making restoration after textual loss part of the parallel."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4b49f53e7b0282d4727e", "ref": "WLC:Jer.36.32", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rewritten scroll with additional words", "note": "After the scroll is burned, Jeremiah dictates again and Baruch writes a replacement with more words; this gives the commentary's movement from loss to recollection and pages a concrete recovery narrative."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3a68e0a51617aadbecb7", "ref": "WLC:Jer.36.32", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "former scroll restored with additional words", "note": "After the burning, Baruch writes the former words from Jeremiah's mouth and many similar words are added; this distinct final step makes remembered speech both recoverable and expandable in a new scroll."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4aab927166aca1e4c250", "ref": "WLC:Jer.36.4", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Jeremiah dictates; Baruch writes", "note": "The commentary's reading-to-pages sequence is enacted when Jeremiah speaks God's words and Baruch writes them on a scroll."}
{"connection_id": "BC-65f9981e5e4f15b0336d", "ref": "WLC:Jer.36.4", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Baruch writes from Jeremiah's mouth", "note": "Baruch writes the divine words from Jeremiah's mouth on a scroll, a particularly close scene of mediated recital becoming pages."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8c205972ab020e7eb1f2", "ref": "WLC:Job.19.23", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Job wishes his words written in a book", "note": "The commentary traces recitation into written pages; Job asks for his own words to be recorded in a book, a lament's appeal for durable preservation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7797fe99f2b0dce37f65", "ref": "WLC:Job.19.23", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Job desires his words inscribed permanently", "note": "Job's wish that his words be written in a book makes durable inscription the response to threatened loss in a non-prophetic setting, extending the commentary's concern with pages that hold what speech might otherwise lose."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4620e37f7c2071ad89a8", "ref": "WLC:Job.19.24", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "words engraved in rock with iron and lead", "note": "The commentary says the written pages preserve what memory might lose; Job intensifies the wish by asking for words cut permanently into rock."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d57c47012b3a86c97a96", "ref": "WLC:Josh.1.8", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "book in the mouth and day-night meditation", "note": "Joshua is told that the Torah book must not depart from his mouth and must be meditated on continually, combining a book, oral repetition, and obedient retention."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ec5680ca03a2b96118e3", "ref": "WLC:Mal.3.16", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew זכרון (zikaron, remembrance)", "note": "The commentary makes dhikr both preservation and remembrance; Malachi uses the cognate in a book written for those who fear the Lord and honor his name, joining remembered devotion to a written record."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c116003068a2a2660f1a", "ref": "WLC:Mal.3.16", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fearers, the Lord's name, book of remembrance", "note": "The commentary says the one who fears will remember and that remembrance of the Lord's name leads to prayer; Malachi's fearers honor the name and are recorded in a book of remembrance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0630fe43af7fd1774945", "ref": "WLC:Mal.3.16", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "book of remembrance for those who fear the Name", "note": "Those who fear YHWH are recorded in a book of remembrance before him, bringing together fear, the divine Name, remembering, and a written record as in 87:9-10 and 15-19."}
{"connection_id": "BC-caef9b83bd59b5ba408f", "ref": "WLC:Mal.3.18", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "distinction between those serving God and those not", "note": "The commentary separates the one who fears and remembers from the one who avoids reminder; Malachi says a coming distinction will be made between the righteous and the wicked."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6c92ce2b9198b3144ded", "ref": "WLC:Mal.3.22", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "remember the Torah of Moses", "note": "The closing command to remember Moses' Torah makes remembrance itself the mode by which a Mosaic written legacy remains operative."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bf585ef74694315cb77f", "ref": "WLC:Neh.8.8", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "reading the Law with explanation so people understand", "note": "The commentary treats reminder as an aid to recover needed knowledge; Nehemiah describes public reading of the book with explanation that makes its meaning clear to listeners."}
{"connection_id": "BC-03421cab7a71064607db", "ref": "WLC:Neh.8.8", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew q-r-ʾ / Arabic q-r-ʾ", "note": "Hebrew q-r-ʾ is used for public reading of God's written Torah with explanation; its formal cognacy with the commentary's قرأ makes 87:6's recitation a textual and communal act, not only private memory."}
{"connection_id": "BC-56f34d41c6b844af747a", "ref": "WLC:Num.15.39", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "visible fringe as a reminder to obey", "note": "The tassel is to be seen so Israel remembers and performs the commands; it gives a material tadhkira-like aid to recall."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d50d3e2f9669d2e70d8f", "ref": "WLC:Prov.3.1", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "do not forget teaching; let the heart keep commands", "note": "The commentary opposes forgetting to keeping entrusted teaching; Proverbs pairs an explicit command not to forget with the heart's custody of instruction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-48bbf75327105a7e5f84", "ref": "WLC:Prov.3.1", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "do not forget teaching; heart keeps commands", "note": "Forgetting Torah is opposed to the heart's keeping commandments, closely matching the commentary's definition of forgetting as failure to hold what was entrusted."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1c8082aae206a50967e7", "ref": "WLC:Prov.3.3", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "teaching written on the heart", "note": "The commentary moves between memory and physical pages; Proverbs says to bind fidelity around the neck and write it on the heart, relocating inscription inside the learner."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9904a0d593587b96b174", "ref": "WLC:Ps.119.11", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "word stored in the heart to prevent sin", "note": "The commentary depicts teaching retained rather than lost; the psalmist says he has stored God's word in his heart so he will not sin."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a4037e1456c24a78d910", "ref": "WLC:Ps.119.16", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "I will not forget your word", "note": "The psalmist's pledge not to forget God's word is the direct devotional counterpart to the commentary's contrast between forgetting and preserving."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bf15456fe251f53d56f8", "ref": "WLC:Ps.119.176", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "lost sheep; seek your servant; I have not forgotten", "note": "The commentary defines recollection as seeking what has gone missing; the psalmist asks God to seek him as a lost sheep while affirming that he remembers God's commandments."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e7105e1bbfaa2033f2a5", "ref": "WLC:Ps.119.93", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "never forgetting precepts that preserve life", "note": "The commentary says remembrance is the opposite of forgetting and that remembered teaching is kept; the psalmist says God's precepts preserve life and therefore will not be forgotten."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8fd4224b1ad2d891d761", "ref": "WLC:Ps.78.7", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "remember God's works and keep commandments", "note": "The commentary says remembrance preserves teaching; the psalm's intergenerational instruction aims to make children hope in God, not forget his works, and keep his commands."}
{"connection_id": "BC-461387eb456a2a0428e1", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 2:2", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "the speakers deny return after death", "note": "The commentary affirms an afterlife better and more lasting than present life; Wisdom's speakers argue that death has no return, directly opposing that horizon."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4cdbd05e3c8d3ef1bfad", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 2:6", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "the speakers urge enjoyment of present goods", "note": "The commentary says people prefer present worldly life although the afterlife is more lasting; Wisdom's speakers instead propose enjoying the good things available now, voicing the contrary valuation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6ea3d91cb92ea953a349", "ref": "b. Bava Batra 14b", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Moses' authorship and the catalog of biblical books", "note": "The rabbinic discussion that attributes Moses' book and other works to named authors is a later Jewish way of organizing the textual corpus behind the commentary's named Mosaic pages."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7396539bf4b312e570cb", "ref": "b. Gittin 60a", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Torah given scroll by scroll or as one whole", "note": "The rabbinic dispute over separate scrolls versus a completed Torah directly frames the commentary's progression from written sheets to a gathered codex as a question about revelation's material form."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a91ef96bd188a791bde2", "ref": "b. Hagigah 9b", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "one hundred versus one hundred and one reviews", "note": "The contrast between reviewing a lesson one hundred and one times versus one hundred times supplies a rabbinic practice of repetition aimed at preventing the loss the commentary calls forgetting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f2a972831d7259259691", "ref": "b. Kiddushin 82a", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Abraham fulfilled Torah before Sinai", "note": "By reading Genesis 26:5 as Abraham's observance of the whole Torah before its giving to Moses, the tradition supplies a Jewish bridge to 87:19's Abraham-and-Moses pairing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c4ff8e7dcac544d496bc", "ref": "b. Pesachim 117b", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "remember Sabbath over wine", "note": "The rabbinic derivation of Sabbath remembrance over wine interprets the Hebrew cognate z-k-r as enacted recall, sharpening the liturgical side of the commentary's ذ ك ر."}

